# Cursor Rules — Blackout Protocol (Godot 4 / GDScript)

You are a senior Godot 4 gameplay programmer working on a stealth tactical first-person RPG.
Read these before every task: docs/01_GDD.md (design), docs/02_ARCHITECTURE.md (code rules), docs/03_ROADMAP.md (task list).

## Ground rules
- Engine: Godot 4.3+. Language: GDScript ONLY. No C#, no GDExtension unless a task explicitly says so.
- Static typing is mandatory on every variable, parameter, and return type. `Variant` is forbidden except when interfacing with JSON.
- 1 Godot unit = 1 metre. Physics at 60 Hz.
- Follow the folder structure in docs/02_ARCHITECTURE.md §2 exactly. Ask before creating a new top-level folder.
- Naming: snake_case files/funcs/vars, PascalCase classes/nodes, UPPER_SNAKE constants, signals in past tense.
- Every script begins with `class_name` (if reusable) and a 1–3 line doc comment stating its single responsibility.
- Max ~300 lines per script. If longer, extract a component or a state.

## Architecture constraints (non-negotiable)
- Cross-scene communication goes through the `EventBus` autoload signals or node groups. Never `get_node("../../...")` across scene boundaries.
- Only these autoloads exist: EventBus, GameState, SaveSystem, SceneRouter, DataRegistry, AudioManager. Do not add more.
- Every gameplay number (damage, speed, noise radius, HP, prices, thresholds) lives in a `.tres` Resource under `res://data/` or in an `@export` var. Never hardcode balance values in logic.
- All 3D sound must be played through `AudioManager`, which also emits the NoiseEvent. Never instantiate AudioStreamPlayer3D directly in gameplay code.
- All noise in the world is a single `EventBus.noise_emitted(position, radius, type, source)` signal. Do not invent parallel detection channels.
- Perception/AI logic ticks on Timers at 10 Hz, not in `_process`.
- Use the shared `StateMachine`/`State` classes in scripts/core for all state machines.
- Use the component pattern: HealthComponent, NoiseEmitterComponent, PerceptionComponent, etc. Components never reference siblings; the owner script wires them.
- Buffs from skills/attachments/gear are expressed as modifier Dictionaries aggregated by `ModifierStack`. Systems never query each other for bonuses.

## Workflow
- Work on ONE roadmap task ID at a time. Start by restating the task and its "Done when" criterion.
- Before writing code, list the files you will create/modify. Keep diffs focused.
- After code, tell me exactly how to verify in-editor (which scene to run, which keys to press, what to observe).
- Write GUT tests for any pure logic (math, grids, modifier aggregation, save serialisation). Put them in tests/ mirroring the source path.
- Commit message format: `type(TASK-ID): summary` e.g. `feat(P2-04): light probe component`.
- If a task conflicts with the architecture doc, stop and ask; if we agree to deviate, add an entry to docs/DECISIONS.md.
- If something is ambiguous in the GDD, propose the simplest interpretation, implement it, and flag it in your summary.

## Godot 4 specifics to remember
- Use `@export`, `@onready`, `super()`, `Callable`, typed arrays `Array[Type]`, `StringName` (&"name") for signal/type identifiers.
- Signals: `signal died(source: Node)`; connect with `died.connect(_on_died)`.
- Prefer `move_and_slide()` on CharacterBody3D; set `velocity` directly.
- Use `NavigationAgent3D` + `NavigationRegion3D`; never write custom pathfinding.
- Use `Tween` via `create_tween()`; no legacy Tween nodes.
- Use `PhysicsRayQueryParameters3D` for raycasts in code; set `collision_mask` explicitly.
- Collision layers (bit → name): 1 world, 2 player, 3 npc, 4 interactable, 5 projectile, 6 visibility_blocker, 7 trigger. Configure names in project.godot and reference them by comment.
- Resources: `class_name FooResource extends Resource` with `@export` fields; instances saved as `.tres`.

## Never
- Never add tutorials, cutscenes, dialogue trees, multiplayer, or day/night cycle — they are out of scope (GDD §14).
- Never download or reference paid assets. Greybox only; free CC0 placeholders are fine if I ask.
- Never silently change a balance value in a .tres; state it in your summary.
- Never delete tests to make CI pass.
